{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Southern Pacific's Coast Daylight, the historical predecessor of Amtrak's Coast Starlight over the Coast Line south of San Francisco. Route geometry is taken from the modern Amtrak route's own GTFS-sourced alignment for this corridor (see amtrak.geojson), since the historical train ran the same physical line; any place where the historical routing differed is described in the segment notes and reflected in the geometry. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones (train introductions, renamings, mergers, discontinuation dates); headway_min and avg_speed_kmh within each era are estimates, not from real timetables.", "source": "Wikipedia (Coast Daylight), american-rails.com, transportationhistory.org", "pulled": "20260817", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Southern Pacific Railroad", "line": "Southern Pacific: Coast Daylight", "id": "sp-coast-daylight", "color": "#C1272D", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 78, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1937, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Coast Daylight began Mar 21 1937, an all-streamlined red/orange/black train hauled by GS-class steam, running the 471 miles between San Francisco and Los Angeles in 9h45m; within a few years it had the highest ridership of any train in the country. North of San Jose it ran the SP Peninsula line into San Francisco proper (today's Caltrain corridor) rather than Amtrak's modern East Bay routing to Oakland, and the geometry here follows that historical Peninsula alignment. Amtrak took over May 1 1971 and rerouted the service to Oakland as today's Coast Starlight - see amtrak.geojson. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Coast Daylight), american-rails.com, transportationhistory.org"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.90956, 37.34002], "to": [-122.1636, 37.4419], "chord_mi": 15.95, "traced_mi": 15.85, "snap_m": [6, 73], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Caltrain Peninsula Subdivision (15.81 mi)", "Union Pacific Railroad (0.04 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.1636, 37.4419], "to": [-122.3255, 37.563], "chord_mi": 12.38, "traced_mi": 12.3, "snap_m": [73, 479], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Caltrain Peninsula Subdivision (12.3 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.61663, 37.09637], "to": [-121.751, 37.22609], "chord_mi": 11.42, "traced_mi": 11.68, "snap_m": [34, 63], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Coast Subdivision (11.68 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.3255, 37.563], "to": [-122.3955, 37.7766], "chord_mi": 15.28, "traced_mi": 17.84, "snap_m": [479, 29], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Caltrain Peninsula Subdivision (17.55 mi)", "4th & King Track 1 (0.15 mi)", "Bay Subdivision (Lead to Downtown Extension Project) (0.11 mi)", "Bay Subdivision (Crossover to Downtown Extension Project) (0.03 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-118.32126, 34.18631], "to": [-118.5915,